window.docContents[178].resolve({"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___6___-Adjoint-functors/Uniqueness-of-adjoints-and-analogy-with-linear-algebra/#Chapter7___Discussion_after_Definition7___6___1":{"contents":"Not every functor has a left or right adjoint, but if it does, it is unique and can be constructed canonically (i.e., if we somehow found two such functors, then there is a canonical isomorphism between them). This follows easily from the Yoneda lemma, since if\nTable 1. Dictionary between category theory and linear algebra.\n\n* Category theory* Linear algebra\n* Category \\mathcal{C}* Vector space V with a nondegenerate inner product\n* The set of morphisms \\operatorname{Hom}(X, Y)* Inner product (x, y) on V (maybe nonsymmetric)\n* Opposite category \\mathcal{C}^{\\mathrm{op}}* Same space V with reversed inner product\n* The category Sets* The ground field k\n* Full subcategory in \\mathcal{C}* Nondegenerate subspace in V\n* Functor F : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{D}* Linear operator f : V \\to W\n* Functor F : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathbf{Sets}* Linear functional f \\in V^* = \\operatorname{Hom}(V, k)\n* Representable functor* Linear functional f \\in V^* given by f(v) = (u, v), u \\in V\n* Yoneda lemma* Nondegeneracy of the inner product (on both sides)\n* Not all functors are representable* If \\dim V = \\infty, not \\forall f \\in V^*, f(v) = (u, v)\n* Left and right adjoint functors* Left and right adjoint operators\n* Adjoint functors don't always exist* Adjoint operators may not exist if \\dim V = \\infty\n* If they do, they are unique* If they do, they are unique\n* Left and right adjoints may not coincide* The inner product may be nonsymmetric\n\nF, G are a pair of adjoint functors, then F(X) represents the functor Y \\mapsto \\operatorname{Hom}(X, G(Y)) and G(Y) represents the functor X \\mapsto \\operatorname{Hom}(F(X), Y).\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t7. Introduction to categories\t7.6. Adjoint functors\tUniqueness of adjoints and analogy with linear algebra","header":"Uniqueness of adjoints and analogy with linear algebra","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___6___-Adjoint-functors/Uniqueness-of-adjoints-and-analogy-with-linear-algebra/#Chapter7___Discussion_after_Definition7___6___1"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___10___-Representations-of-tensor-products/Irreducible-representations-of-tensor-products-of-algebras/#Chapter3___Theorem3___10___2":{"contents":"Theorem 3.10.2. (i) Let V be an irreducible finite dimensional representation of A and let W be an irreducible finite dimensional representation of B. Then V \\otimes W is an irreducible representation of A \\otimes B.\n\n(ii) Any irreducible finite dimensional representation M of A \\otimes B has the form (i) for unique V and W.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t3. General results of representation theory\t3.10. Representations of tensor products\tIrreducible representations of tensor products of algebras","header":"Irreducible representations of tensor products of algebras","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___10___-Representations-of-tensor-products/Irreducible-representations-of-tensor-products-of-algebras/#Chapter3___Theorem3___10___2"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___12___-The-tensor-algebra/Symmetric-algebra___-exterior-algebra___-and-universal-enveloping-algebra-_LPAR_coordinate-free_RPAR_/#Chapter2___Definition2___12___1":{"contents":"x_1, \\ldots, x_N in V defines an isomorphism of TV with the free algebra k\\langle x_1, \\ldots, x_n \\rangle.\n\nAlso, one can make the following definition.\n\nDefinition 2.12.1. (i) The symmetric algebra SV of V is the quotient of TV by the ideal generated by v \\otimes w - w \\otimes v, v, w \\in V.\n\n(ii) The exterior algebra \\wedge V of V is the quotient of TV by the ideal generated by v \\otimes v, v \\in V.\n\n(iii) If V is a Lie algebra, the universal enveloping algebra \\mathcal{U}(V) of V is the quotient of TV by the ideal generated by v \\otimes w - w \\otimes v - [v, w], v, w \\in V.\n\nIt is easy to see that a choice of a basis x_1, \\ldots, x_N in V identifies SV with the polynomial algebra k[x_1, \\ldots, x_N], \\wedge V with the exterior algebra \\wedge_k(x_1, \\ldots, x_N), and the universal enveloping algebra \\mathcal{U}(V) with one defined previously.\n\nMoreover, it is easy to see that we have decompositions\n\nSV = \\bigoplus_{n \\geq 0} S^n V, \\quad \\wedge V = \\bigoplus_{n \\geq 0} \\wedge^n V.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t2. Basic notions of representation theory\t2.12. The tensor algebra\tSymmetric algebra, exterior algebra, and universal enveloping algebra (coordinate-free)","header":"Symmetric algebra, exterior algebra, and universal enveloping algebra (coordinate-free)","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___12___-The-tensor-algebra/Symmetric-algebra___-exterior-algebra___-and-universal-enveloping-algebra-_LPAR_coordinate-free_RPAR_/#Chapter2___Definition2___12___1"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/Chapter-8___-Homological-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--Chapter-8___-Homological-algebra":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t8. Homological algebra","header":"Chapter 8: Homological algebra","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/Chapter-8___-Homological-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--Chapter-8___-Homological-algebra"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/#chapter-03___section-3-1":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t3. General results of representation theory","header":"3.1. Subrepresentations in semisimple representations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/#chapter-03___section-3-1"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___3___-Examples/#chapter-04___section-4-3":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t4. Representations of finite groups: Basic results","header":"4.3. Examples","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___3___-Examples/#chapter-04___section-4-3"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___7___-Orthogonality-of-matrix-elements/Section-4___7___-Orthogonality-of-matrix-elements/#Chapter4___Introduction_4___7":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t4. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\t4.7. Orthogonality of matrix elements\tSection 4.7: Orthogonality of matrix elements","header":"Section 4.7: Orthogonality of matrix elements","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___7___-Orthogonality-of-matrix-elements/Section-4___7___-Orthogonality-of-matrix-elements/#Chapter4___Introduction_4___7"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___4___-Roots/Remark-6___4___11___-Weyl-group/#Chapter6___Remark6___4___11":{"contents":"Remark 6.4.11. As a linear operator of \\mathbb{R}^n, s_\\alpha fixes any vector orthogonal to \\alpha and\n\ns_\\alpha(\\alpha) = -\\alpha.\n\nTherefore s_\\alpha is the reflection at the hyperplane orthogonal to \\alpha and in particular fixes B. The s_i := s_{\\alpha_i} generate a subgroup W \\subseteq O(\\mathbb{R}^n), which is called the Weyl group of \\Gamma. Since for every w \\in W, w(\\alpha_i) is a root, and since there are only finitely many roots, W has to be finite.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t6. Quiver representations\t6.4. Roots\tRemark 6.4.11: Weyl group","header":"Remark 6.4.11: Weyl group","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___4___-Roots/Remark-6___4___11___-Weyl-group/#Chapter6___Remark6___4___11"}});
